The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!timing_np) {
    pr_err("%pOF: could not find node '%s'\n", np, name);
}
```
Reason:
The unconditional error message `pr_err()` was being printed when the timing node was not found, which was unnecessary in cases where a fallback mechanism was available. This created confusion in the logs, indicating an error even when the absence of the node was acceptable.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!timing_np)
    return -ENOENT;
```
Reason:
By removing the error message and returning `-ENOENT` instead, the patch allows the function to indicate that the timing node was not found without cluttering the logs with unnecessary error messages. This change enables individual drivers to handle their own error reporting as needed, improving the overall error handling mechanism.

